To make this code concurrent, we can use a goroutine to handle each request, and a channel to receive incoming requests. We can modify the code as follows:
```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"net/http"
)

func handleRequest(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
	// Fetch data from database
	data := fetchDataFromDatabase(r.URL.Path)

	// Send response back to client
	fmt.Fprintf(w, "Data: %s", data)
}

func fetchDataFromDatabase(path string) string {
	// Simulate database query
	return "Data from database"
}

func main() {
	reqChan := make(chan *http.Request)

	go func() {
		for {
			select {
			case req := <-reqChan:
				go handleRequest(req)
			}
		}
	}()

	http.HandleFunc("/", func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
		reqChan <- r
	})

	http.ListenAndServe(":8080", nil)
}
```
In this example, we use a channel to receive incoming requests, and a goroutine to handle each request. This allows the server to respond quickly to multiple requests.

