One common mistake that learners make when writing concurrent programs is to use shared variables without proper synchronization. This can lead to data corruption and other concurrency-related bugs. To avoid this, it's essential to use synchronization primitives, such as mutexes and semaphores, to protect shared resources from concurrent access.

